iven board \\(n=2025\\), the answer is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4050\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider an \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(j\\)).  \nMatilda places axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines,\ntiles are pairwise interior\u2011disjoint, and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square and each column also\ncontains exactly one uncovered square, the set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nof uncovered squares is determined by a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Thus \\(|U|=n\\) and the remaining \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares must be tiled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Decomposition into \u201cleft\u2019\u2019 and \u201cright\u2019\u2019 parts  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) let the uncovered square be at column \\(\\pi(i)\\).\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of all squares **left** of the uncovered squares,\n\\(R\\) of those **right** of them.  Clearly \\(L\\cup R\\) is the set of squares\nthat have to be tiled, and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A Ferrers board and a lower bound  \n\nRe\u2011order the rows according to the values \\(\\pi(i)\\): let\n\\(r_{1},r_{2},\\dots ,r_{n}\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(r_{1})<\\pi(r_{2})<\\dots <\\pi(r_{n}).\n\\]\n\nIf we write the rows of \\(L\\) in this order, the shape of \\(L\\) becomes a\nFerrers (Young) diagram: the \\(k\\)-th row (in the new order) contains exactly\n\\(\\pi(r_{k})-1\\) squares, and these numbers are non\u2011decreasing with \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(B\\) be a Ferrers board with \\(n\\) rows, each row non\u2011empty.\nAny tiling of \\(B\\) by axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn column \\(c\\) of a Ferrers board the highest occupied cell lies in row\n\\(c+1\\) (because the row lengths are non\u2011decreasing).  \nConsequently, a rectangle that contains a cell of column \\(c\\) must have its\ntop side in a row \\(\\ge c+1\\).  \nIf two different columns \\(c_{1}<c_{2}\\) were covered by the same rectangle,\nits top side would have to be simultaneously \\(\\ge c_{1}+1\\) and \\(\\ge\nc_{2}+1\\), i.e. \\(\\ge c_{2}+1\\); then the rectangle would miss the highest\ncell of column \\(c_{1}\\).  Hence each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) forces a\ndifferent rectangle.  Thus at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary. \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 to the Ferrers board obtained from \\(L\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument applied to the right part \\(R\\) (after reversing the\norder of the rows) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint,\n(1)\u2013(2) imply\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling needs at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThe uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Left (lower\u2011triangular) region.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle; the family\n\\(\\{T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) exactly fills all squares with\nrow\u202f>\u202fcolumn, i.e. the set \\(L\\).  The rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Right (upper\u2011triangular) region.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) rectangles; together they fill all squares with\nrow\u202f<\u202fcolumn, i.e. the set \\(R\\).  Again the rectangles are disjoint.\n\nThus we have constructed exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles that cover every\nsquare except the \\(n\\) uncovered diagonal squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles,\nwhile the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Application to the original board  \n\nFor the given \\(2025\\times2025\\) board (\\(n=2025\\))\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4050}\n\\]"
