or column \\(n\\) place a single tile covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n\n   All tiles are disjoint, every unit square except the \\(n\\) holes is covered, and the total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)\\times 2 + 1 + 1 = 2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the grid be an \\(n\\times n\\) board with \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote by \\((i,j)\\) the unit square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(j\\) \\((1\\le i,j\\le n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Structure forced by the holes\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square,\nthe set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a\nbijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered\nsquare in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nFix a column \\(c\\).  \nLet \\(r=\\pi^{-1}(c)\\) be the row of the hole in this column.\nThe column is split into two (possibly empty) parts:\n\n* the *upper part* \\(U_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i< r\\}\\),\n* the *lower part* \\(L_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid r< i\\le n\\}\\).\n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain the hole \\((r,c)\\); consequently a single\ntile cannot cover squares from both \\(U_c\\) and \\(L_c\\).  \nThus **every column whose hole is not in the topmost or bottommost row\nrequires at least two distinct tiles**, one covering \\(U_c\\) and one covering\n\\(L_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nOnly one hole may lie in the top row (row 1) because each row contains exactly\none hole. Hence at most one column has its hole in the top row; call this\ncolumn \\(c_{\\text{top}}\\) (if it exists).  \nAnalogously, at most one column has its hole in the bottom row (row \\(n\\));\ncall it \\(c_{\\text{bottom}}\\) (if it exists).\n\nConsequently at least \\(n-2\\) columns have their holes strictly inside the\ncolumn (neither top nor bottom).  \nEach of those \\(n-2\\) columns needs **at least two** tiles, while the two\nexceptional columns need **at least one** tile each. Therefore any admissible\ntiling uses at least\n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-2)+1+1 = 2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThus the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).\n\n* Column 1: its hole is at \\((1,1)\\) (the top row).  \n  Cover the remaining squares of this column with a single vertical tile\n  of size \\((n-1)\\times1\\) occupying rows \\(2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n* Column \\(n\\): its hole is at \\((n,n)\\) (the bottom row).  \n  Cover the remaining squares of this column with a single vertical tile\n  of size \\((n-1)\\times1\\) occupying rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) the hole is at \\((j,j)\\), a\n  interior position.  \n  Place two vertical tiles:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\text{Tile }T^{\\text{up}}_j :\\; \\text{rows }1,\\dots ,j-1,\\ \\text{column }j,\n  &&\\text{size }(j-1)\\times1,\\\\\n  &\\text{Tile }T^{\\text{down}}_j :\\; \\text{rows }j+1,\\dots ,n,\\ \\text{column }j,\n  &&\\text{size }(n-j)\\times1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\nAll these tiles are disjoint (they lie in distinct columns), they cover every\nunit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes, and their total number is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{column }1}\n+\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{column }n}\n+\\underbrace{2\\,(n-2)}_{\\text{columns }2,\\dots ,n-1}\n=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower‑bound argument\nsho