st column of **some** rectangle, because a rectangle cannot skip a column without also containing a hole. Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the left part.\n\n   An analogous argument applied to the cells right of the holes shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the right part.\n\n   Hence any tiling of the board that leaves exactly one uncovered square in each row and column uses **at least** \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the counting argument shows that fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles are impossible. Therefore the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\n   Substituting \\(n=2025\\) yields the final answer \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) board by coordinates \\((r,c)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\).  \n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a **permutation matrix**:\nthere exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered\nsquares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles whose\ninteriors are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nRe‑order the rows so that the hole‑columns appear in increasing order.\nFormally, let \\(\\pi\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\pi(1))<\\sigma(\\pi(2))<\\dots <\\sigma(\\pi(n)).\n\\]\n\nFor brevity write \\(c_k=\\sigma(\\pi(k))\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nc_1<c_2<\\dots <c_n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Left part.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\bigl\\{(\\,r,c\\,)\\mid r\\in\\{\\pi(j+1),\\dots ,\\pi(n)\\},