\\mid r\\in\\{\\pi(j+1),\\dots ,\\pi(n)\\},\n\\;c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,c_j-1\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c_j<c_{j+1}\\le\\cdots\\le c_n\\), every row used in \\(L_j\\) has its hole\nstrictly to the right of column \\(c_j-1\\); consequently \\(L_j\\) contains no\nhole. The rectangles \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their\nunion equals the set of all squares that lie **left** of the hole in their\nrow.\n\n---\n\n**Right part.**  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\bigl\\{(\\,r,c\\,)\\mid r\\in\\{\\pi(1),\\dots ,\\pi(j)\\},\n\\;c\\in\\{c_{j+1}+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow each used row has its hole strictly to the left of column \\(c_{j+1}+1\\),\nso \\(R_j\\) also avoids all holes. The rectangles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) are\ndisjoint and together cover every square that lies **right** of its row\u2019s\nhole.\n\n---\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{R_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) tile\nexactly the complement of \\(U\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\nAgain order the rows so that the hole\u2011columns increase:\n\\(c_1<c_2<\\dots <c_n\\).\n\nConsider the set of squares **left** of the holes:\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(r,c)\\mid r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;c<c_{\\text{row}(r)}\\}.\n\\]\n\nAfter the ordering, \\(L\\) has the shape of a Young diagram (a staircase):\nits rightmost occupied column is \\(c_{n-1}-1\\); the next distinct rightmost\ncolumn is \\(c_{n-2}-1\\), and so on, down to column \\(c_{1}-1\\).  \nThus there are exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct column indices that appear as the\nrightmost column of some cell of \\(L\\).\n\nA rectangle that lies completely inside \\(L\\) cannot contain two of these\nrightmost columns without also containing a hole, because any column larger\nthan the hole\u2011column of a row would intersect that hole.